for centuries  it looked as if religion was trying to  tell us facts  about the world and just got things a  bit wrong  for example how old the earth was it  claimed 4 000 years old  or how many sons there were in the  universe it claimed  one but in reality religion was never  really interested  in doing the sorts of things that  science does  it might have thrown off the odd theory  but at heart it cared about a mission  altogether different  it wanted to tell us stories to make  life  feel more bearable it was interested in  giving us something to hold on to  that could help us to make it through to  the next day  at the same time science properly viewed  has never been the enemy of spiritual  enrichment  it can yield ideas every bit as  consoling  and inspiring as those found in religion  we can usefully look to science for the  sort of ideas  that we used to seek in religion here  are four  big consoling ideas that can be found  in science firstly perspective  we are at permanent risk in the  conditions of modern life  of losing perspective that is of making  more of our troubles and fears than is  good for  us one of the great benefits of science  is that it can help us to feel small  science teaches us that our galaxy the  milky way  has approximately 100 billion stars in  it  that there are 10 billion galaxies in  the observable universe  each of which contains an average of a  hundred billion stars  which means that there are around a  billion trillion stars out there  when we lose perspective as we  invariably do  in the course of pretty much every day  in the frenetic city  we should spend a few moments with a  photograph  from the hubble telescope and remember  that we are  in a glorious and redemptive way what we  always feared nothing  secondly all is vanity  many of our efforts are designed to  perpetuate ourselves in time we strive  to live on through our work  and to make something more enduring than  our biological selves  to release us from this exhausting and  vainglorious folly  religions used to kindly remind us in  the words of ecclesiastes  that all is vanity science offers us  a yet more powerful expression of this  biblical concept  the second law of thermodynamics  this states that the tendency of all  systems of which the universe is one  is to dissipate energy over time until  it reaches  a state of complete rest given a  sufficient span  our universe and its superclusters of  galaxies will all collapse  and we will enter what scientists call a  dark era  in which after so much excitement  individual and cosmic  nothing will remain except for a dilute  quiet  gas of photons and leptons the situation  is no better closer to home  in about four billion years when the sun  runs out of hydrogen  it will become a red giant star possibly  expanding as far as mars  at which point it will absorb and  destroy earth  to repeat the point but this time with  nasa's help  all truly is vanity  thirdly forgiveness it's often tempting  to lose our temper with ourselves and  our fellow humans  why can't we be more reasonable why are  we so prejudiced  why are we so prone to anxiety why do we  eat so much why are we so interested in  pornography  it's equally tempting to search for  explanations that emphasize our  villainous natures and then to harshly  condemn  our lack of self-command we end up  disgusted with ourselves and  judgmental towards others but science  arguably more effectively than religion  can teach us the art of forgiveness  and liberate us from our urge to  criticize  of course we are less than ideally  adapted to the civilized and  complex lives we aspire to lead we've  had  very little time to do anything else  science tells us that we appeared in  more or less our current form  in africa two hundred thousand years ago  for most of this time we lived in small  groups  we foraged we grunted we didn't wait for  others to stop  talking we fought constantly and we were  terrified  of everything the time since the birth  of jesus  comprises one percent of our history  the last 250 years the period since we  became urbanized and began  living with technology encompasses a  mere  naught point one percent naturally  therefore  most of our impulses are going to be  better suited to more basic conditions  it's a miracle frankly that we ever  manage to be polite  to explain our feelings or to see it  from another's point of view  we are from the vantage point of science  doing  extremely well indeed evolutionary  history  teaches us that humans should be a lot  worse than they are  the wonder isn't in the end that we're  so uncivilized  but that we ever even manage now and  then to have  a few moments of civilization  fourthly our existence  science is supremely capable of  nurturing feelings of gratitude  because of a basic truth about the way  gratitude  works it stems from an awareness  of how much more awful things might have  been  and here when it comes to our life on  the planet  science tells us that we have so much to  be grateful for  for example we can be grateful that 13.8  billion years ago  something smaller than an electron chose  to swell  within a fraction of a second like an  expanding balloon  into a zone permeated with energy 93  billion light years in size  that we now clumsily call the universe  we can be grateful that some of the  energy from this swift expansion  was able to coagulate into particles  which group together to form light atoms  of  hydrogen lithium and helium which then  assembled into galaxies  which gave birth to stars inside whose  molten burning cores  all the elements necessary for the  nucleic acids essential to life  were forged we can be grateful that  gravity  drew these stars together to create  galaxies 100 billion of them  including fortuitously the milky way a  small corner of the universe containing  just  400 billion stars in which our sun was  born  out of a giant spinning cloud of dust  and gas  4.5 billion years ago we can be grateful  that around the same time  swarms of debris collided to form our  earth  a lava-washed uninhabitable rock that  gravity happened to throw into orbit  as the third planet from the sun the  exact right distance for life to develop  we can be grateful that another planet  thea collided with earth  gifting us our moon which slowed the  earth's rotation  stabilized atmospheric conditions and  created the 24-hour day  and caused the earth to tilt forming the  seasons  we can be grateful that ice particles  left over from the collisions of  hundreds of comets melted water vapor  condensed  and oceans were formed we can be  grateful that comet collisions delivered  another chance cosmic gift  the essential components of life and dna  like ribose carbon dioxide ethanol  amino acids and phosphorus we can be  grateful that underwater hot springs  release the right amount of energy in  the right mix of chemicals  to allow the first single cell organisms  to form  four billion years ago we can be  grateful that the earth's toxic  atmosphere of  methane and carbon dioxide slowly became  sweetened by the release of oxygen  from cyanobacteria the first creatures  to photosynthesize  and gradually oxygenated 85 percent of  the atmosphere  we can be grateful that an asteroid 15  kilometers wide  happened to hit earth 65.5 million years  ago  and destroyed most terrestrial organisms  including those  non-avian dinosaurs but created ideal  conditions  in which small furry mammals our close  ancestors  were able to thrive with less  competition we can be grateful that your  genes  managed to pass safely through an  unbroken 10  000 generation chain despite the best  efforts of cyclones predators and  a constant barrage of viruses and we can  be grateful  that an average fertile woman will have  a hundred thousand eggs  and a man will produce a trillion sperm  each of these  very different but that nevertheless you  have managed to emerge from all the  options  just as you are and to all this as they  used to say in the churches  one might cry or whisper hallelujah  there is enough in science to give birth  to 20 religions  so much to worship to be awed by and to  be consoled through  things like dark matter string theory or  quantum wave functions  the curse of the modern world is not to  have invented science  it's not yet to have understood all the  amazing things  one might still do with it at the school  of life we run regular virtual classes  for adults  these mini life courses cover such  topics as making relationships work  overcoming anger and anxiety career  guidance  finding meaning and purpose in life and  using culture as a therapeutic  tool click the link on screen now to  learn more

